Infernalist: Chaos On Fire

<p>The Infernalist is the loud, messy cousin of the classic summoner, and it kind of rules. You are not spamming fragile skeletons that pop if a boss sneezes; you are throwing out fire-soaked demons that leave burning ground and chip away at anything that gets close. The big thing you notice after a few maps is how steady the damage feels. You can step out of the arena to avoid some nonsense mechanic, mash a flask because you misread a telegraph, and your minions just keep going, melting the pack for you. They double as tanks too, so early on you are not stuck trying to juggle a shield and a clunky defensive setup just to survive a rare monster with bad mods.</p>

Invoker: More Brains, Same Carnage

<p>Invoker plays very differently, even though it is still all about summons on the field. It is less \"release the hounds and chill\" and more like you are calling plays mid-fight. You are dropping your minions, weaving in your own spells, and repositioning yourself to funnel mobs into choke points. That mix of personal casting plus minion pressure gives you nice control over the flow of a fight, especially in tight layouts. In a group, people usually like having an Invoker around, because your summons soak up random hits and the visual noise actually helps draw attention away from squishy builds. You can also pivot between mapping and bossing with some gem and passive tweaks, without needing to tear the whole tree down and start over.</p>

Why These Builds Feel Safe

<p>Path of Exile 2 hits harder than the first game in a lot of spots, and you feel it as soon as you get into tougher zones. That is where the \"army in front, you in the back\" setup really shines. A wall of minions buys you a second or two to react, and that small buffer can be the difference between cruising through a map and eating a death that wipes a chunk of your XP bar. You will not always delete the screen like some high-end bow builds, at least not at league start, but you get something more important: consistency. Fewer surprise deaths, fewer panic respecs because your build bricked in red maps, and more time actually progressing instead of rage-logging.</p>

